{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB06415 - MONDO_6502",
  "verbalization": "Calfactant is implicated in the pathogenesis of Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) through a chain of indications that link it to respiratory distress in newborns. The evidence suggests that Calfactant is used to treat Newborn Respiratory Distress Syndrome, which in turn is associated with two other drugs—Tyloxapol, Poractant Alfa, and Colfosceril Palmitate—all of which are indicated for ARDS. This indicates a potential pathway where Calfactant may exert a therapeutic effect on ARDS by targeting the underlying pathology of respiratory distress in newborns. The shared indication of these drugs for ARDS suggests a possible mechanism of action that involves modulating respiratory function or reducing inflammation, though the exact mechanism remains unclear. The evidence supports a repurposing of Calfactant for ARDS, but further studies are needed to confirm its efficacy and mechanism of action in this context.",
